<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vengi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vengi is a historical region of coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the deltas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Godavari rivers, named after its old capital near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eluru">Eluru</a>. Conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> around 624, it became the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>, who ruled it until the eleventh century, when it was united with the Chola empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vengi, also called Venginadu, spread over the deltas of the Godavari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. Its capital stood at Pedavegi, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eluru">Eluru</a>. For nearly seven centuries the city was a leading centre of ancient and medieval Andhra and served as the capital of several dynasties, among them the Salankayanas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the mid-3rd century BCE Vengi was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. After the decline of the Mauryas it came under the Satavahana dynasty, which ruled for nearly four centuries, and then under the Pallavas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-ikshvakus">Andhra Ikshvakus</a>. By 300 the Salankayana dynasty had replaced the Andhra Ikshvakus, and in the late 5th century the Vishnukundinas annexed the Salankayana lands and extended their rule over the region.</p><p>In the early 7th century King Pulakesin II of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> took Vengi from the Vishnukundinas and installed his brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubja-vishnuvardhana">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</a>, as its ruler, founding the Eastern Chalukya dynasty. Its kings extended their influence north as far as Srikakulam and south as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a>, and they often faced invasions by the Rashtrakutas and other neighbours.</p><p>In the late 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king Raja Raja Chola I invaded Vengi to support the Eastern Chalukya prince Saktivarman I against local rivals, and Saktivarman was restored to the throne in 1002 under Chola overlordship. Marriages between the two houses followed, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulothunga Chola I</a> the Cholas absorbed Vengi into their empire. After the Chola decline the region passed to the Velanati Chodas, then to the Kakatiya Empire in the early 13th century, the Reddy dynasty from 1328 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> in the 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> were patrons of Telugu literature. From the time of Gunaga Vijayaditya Telugu prose and poetry developed at their court, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a> the court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannaya">Nannaya</a> began the Telugu translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, a milestone in the literature of the language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vengi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pulakeshin II also used a fleet, said in his inscription to number hundreds of ships, to take Puri, the island capital of the Konkan Mauryas, and around 624 he conquered <b>Vengi</b> in the eastern Deccan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Marital alliances bound the Cholas to the Eastern Chalukyas of <b>Vengi</b>, which remained largely independent under Chola protection, and Kulothunga Chola III allied with the Hoysala Veera Ballala II through a marriage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>War with the Chola Empire for the fertile Raichur doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra and for influence over <b>Vengi</b> in coastal Andhra dominated the empire’s history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 624 Pulakeshin II of the Chalukyas of Badami, having conquered the Vishnukundina dynasty, made his younger brother Kubja Vishnuvardhana viceroy of the <b>Vengi</b> region between the Krishna and Godavari rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a></li><li><blockquote>Their capital was <b>Vengi</b>, near modern Eluru.</blockquote><a href="/state/salankayana-dynasty">Salankayana dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 11th century they rose as feudatories of the Chola emperor Kulottunga I, who had absorbed the lands of the Eastern Chalukyas, and governed the <b>Vengi</b> region on his behalf from Chandavolu, near Tenali.</blockquote><a href="/state/velanati-chodas">Velanati Chodas</a></li></ul></section>
